---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../consts';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Affiliate disclosure"
  description="Fader Report currently carries no affiliate links and earns nothing. This page explains what will change if that ever stops being true."
>
  <h1 class="pagetitle">Affiliate disclosure</h1>

  <aside class="box">
    <strong>Current status</strong>
    <p>
      As of today, <strong style="display:inline;text-transform:none;font-size:inherit;color:inherit">Fader Report carries no affiliate links and earns no commission from anything.</strong>
      There is nothing to disclose yet. This page sets out the rules in advance, so they are
      on the record before there is any money involved.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <h2>What an affiliate link is</h2>
  <p>
    A link that pays the site a commission if you buy something after clicking it, at no extra
    cost to you. It is a normal way for a publication like this to survive. It is also an
    obvious way for a publication like this to become useless, if the commission starts
    deciding what gets recommended.
  </p>

  <h2>The rules we are binding ourselves to</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Any page containing affiliate links carries a disclosure box <em>above</em> the content
      it applies to, not buried in the footer.
    </li>
    <li>Affiliate links are marked <code>rel="sponsored nofollow"</code> in the HTML.</li>
    <li>
      Commission rates never affect rankings, recommendations or the order of a comparison. If
      the best option for you pays us nothing, that is still the recommendation, and we will
      say outright that it pays us nothing.
    </li>
    <li>
      We will not accept payment for a review, a placement, a link, or a rewrite. No sponsored
      posts, no “partnerships” that produce coverage.
    </li>
    <li>
      We will not join a programme whose terms require us to hide the relationship or avoid
      criticising the product.
    </li>
    <li>
      Free products stay in comparisons on merit. Open-source tools that pay nobody anything
      will keep being recommended when they are the right answer.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why this page exists now</h2>
  <p>
    Writing the rules while there is no money at stake is easy. That is exactly why it is worth
    doing now rather than later. You can hold us to it — the page history is public, and if
    these rules ever get quietly loosened, that is a thing you can check.
  </p>
  <p>
    How we handle the rest of it is in the
    <a href={withBase('/editorial-policy/')}>editorial policy</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
